const leftExecutive = Q.left_in_government && Q.government_has_confidence &&
    !Q.caretaker_government;
if (leftExecutive) {
    Q.government_delivery -= 2;
    Q.government_coalition_dissent += 2;
} else {
    Q.public_trust -= 2;
}
Q.institutional_trust -= 3;
Q.judicial_legitimacy -= 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "A flagship public-money prosecution collapses on a limitation period";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Impunity does not need a verdict

Nobody was acquitted. The case was reassigned twice between prosecutors,
two witnesses were never examined, an expert report was commissioned eleven
months late, and the limitation period on the central count expired while the
file sat in a registry.

Everything that has been promised about accountability now has a concrete
illustration attached to it: a public-money case that ended without anyone
deciding anything at all. Not an acquittal — an expiry.

The lesson taught to every official watching is exact, and it is not about
guilt. It is that a file can be outlasted.

- @justice_statutory_repair
- @justice_committee
- @justice_publish_files
- @justice_move_on

