@coalition_showdown
# prose-audit: Grade A — The overrule ledger is the event: three named measures cut in the Chancellery rather than a dissent score, and the four responses separate a written procedure, a public ledger, a paid-for surrender and a withdrawal threat that can genuinely fail.
title: Three vetoes in, the ministries ask what they are for
subtitle: The junior partner's overrule ledger becomes a coalition question
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 22
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and formation_in_progress = 0 and left_in_government = 1 and left_is_junior_partner = 1 and ministry_overruled_count >= 3 and coalition_showdown_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.coalition_showdown_done = 1;
Q.pressure_last_event = "Coalition overrule showdown";
Q.left_coalition_dissent += 5;
Q.progressives_dissent += 3;
Q.labor_dissent += 3;
Q.coalition_blur += 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "Lewica's ministers ask the coalition what their portfolios are for";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The overrule ledger

Three measures written in our ministries have been cut, deferred or rewritten
in somebody else's Chancellery. The most recent was **[+
ministry_overruled_last +]**. None of the three was refused in cabinet, because
none of the three reached a vote.

Our ministers are asked at every interview why a portfolio the Left negotiated
for produces announcements rather than statutes. The honest answer — that
**[+ coalition_senior_partner +]** decides which of our drafts exist — is also
the answer that ends the government.

The caucus wants this settled. So, for entirely different reasons, does the
Chancellery.

- @showdown_protocol
- @showdown_publish
- @showdown_compensation
- @showdown_ultimatum

@showdown_protocol
title: Demand a written dispute procedure
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Boring, durable, and the only version that binds the next dispute as well as this one.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Negotiating and defending a procedure requires one resource.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.coalition_dispute_protocol = 1;
Q.coalition_management = "Written dispute protocol after the overrule ledger";
Q.ministry_overruled_count = 0;
Q.government_coalition_dissent -= 4;
Q.left_coalition_dissent -= 5;
Q.government_delivery += 2;
Q.party_unity += 2;
Q.coalition_blur -= 2;
Q.pressure_last_response = "Written dispute procedure";
Q.news_headline =
    "The coalition writes down who may stop whose bill, and how";
!}

The procedure is unglamorous: a fixed period for objections, a named cabinet
committee to hear them, and a rule that a measure not objected to inside the
window proceeds. It does not give us a veto. It removes the informal one.

The Chancellery signs because the alternative is answering the question in
public. Every future dispute now starts from a document rather than from
whoever gets to the Prime Minister first.

- @poland_event_queue: Demand the procedure

